1. What is a Nested List?
A nested list is simply a list inside another list.
Why are nested lists useful?
They are useful when dealing with:
•	Student records.
•	Matrices.
•	Tabular data.
•	Game boards.
•	Two-dimensional data.
Nested indexing works from the outside inward.
Just like normal lists, nested lists are mutable.
How do we print every student's information without manually accessing each student?
We use loops.

The loop accesses each inner list individually.
We can also use a loop inside another loop.
1. What is List Comprehension?
List comprehension provides a concise way to create new lists from existing iterables.
Understanding the Syntax
General syntax:
new_list = [expression for item in iterable]
Component	Meaning
expression	What you want to store
item	Variable representing each element
iterable	Collection being processed
Another syntax
[expression for item in iterable if condition]
Execution:
1.	Take an element.
2.	Check the condition.
3.	If the condition is true, include the element.
4.	Otherwise, ignore it.

The expression num determines what gets added to the resulting list.
Important: Don't sacrifice readability just to write fewer lines. Nested comprehensions can become difficult to understand when they get complicated.
Traditional Loops	List Comprehensions
More explicit	More concise
Easier for complex logic	Excellent for simple transformations
Multiple lines	Often one line
Good for debugging complex operations	Good for readable data processing
